{"title":"Polya’s method for extremums","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Polya’s_method\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Polya’s method</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#The_segment_of_the_shortest_length\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The segment of the shortest length</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#The_circle_inside_a_fixed_plane_angle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The circle inside a fixed plane angle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#The_sphere_inside_a_fixed_trihedral_angle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The sphere inside a fixed trihedral angle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#The_smallest_inscribed_equilateral_triangle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The smallest inscribed equilateral triangle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Curve_of_the_smallest_length\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Curve of the smallest length</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Triangle_of_the_biggest_area\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Triangle of the biggest area</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Clever_point_elephant_in_hollow_tetrahedron\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Clever point elephant in hollow tetrahedron</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span id=\"Polya.E2.80.99s_method\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Polya’s_method\">Polya’s method</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polya%E2%80%99s_method_for_extremums&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Polya’s method\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>People involved in spirituality believe that we are created and placed in the best of all possible worlds.\n</p><p>Therefore, the laws governing our world are based on the principles of optimality.\n</p><p>Natural scientists have found a number of empirical laws and methods that describe nature and therefore optimal laws. \n</p><p>Pólya proposed a method in which the researcher replaces the geometric problem of finding the optimum with a mechanical or optical model and applies empirical dependencies to this model.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_segment_of_the_shortest_length\">The segment of the shortest length</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polya%E2%80%99s_method_for_extremums&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: The segment of the shortest length\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Shortest_segment.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/a/a5/Shortest_segment.png/400px-Shortest_segment.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"380\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/a/a5/Shortest_segment.png/600px-Shortest_segment.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/a/a5/Shortest_segment.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> has the ends on the sides of a right angle and contains a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/b/06b75bc5e9f77c399654d9ef6c3868825ce9ab3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C(x_C, y_C).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"85\" height=\"20\" /> Find the shortest length of such a segment.\n</p><p><i><b>Solution</b></i> \n</p><p>Let's imagine that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is a spring rod that cannot bend, but tends to shorten its length. \n</p><p>The rod is fixed at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> on a hinge without friction. The hinge allows the rod to rotate and slide.\n</p><p>The ends of the rod can slide without friction along the grooves - the sides of the corner. \n</p><p>Let the rod be balanced, and the force pulling it together is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/2/e429d0525390a2f39aff76b978aa88336056b732.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T.$\" width=\"15\" height=\"12\" /> The grooves can create a force only along the normal, so they act on the rod with forces \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/b/56b6720ecda59814d758a3e5ffe509d59e5631f3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[F_B = \\frac {T}{\\sin \\alpha}, F_A = \\frac {T}{\\cos \\alpha}.\\]\" width=\"189\" height=\"37\" /> \nFor the rod to be balanced, it is required that the moments of the forces be equal relative to point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/792daa030c96693198f143f9176bf888b7ca25cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C.$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> The moments of forces are:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/1/2a134280a47ee7546c9007d8351005418027170a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[F_A \\cdot AC \\cdot \\sin \\alpha =  F_B \\cdot BC \\cdot \\cos \\alpha \\implies \\frac {BC}{AC} = \\tan^2 \\alpha,\\]\" width=\"414\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/8/458ac75eb7d406401551578764f66df5453f2ad6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac {y_C}{x_C} = \\frac {BC \\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{AC \\cdot \\cos \\alpha} = \\tan^3 \\alpha \\implies\\]\" width=\"243\" height=\"40\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/d/a2da672330476d2268ef1a12a2e7638bb8ab7f96.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AB = \\left ( x_C^ {\\frac {2}{3}} + y_C^ {\\frac {2}{3}}\\right ) ^{\\frac {3}{2}}.\\]\" width=\"154\" height=\"37\" />\n<b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_circle_inside_a_fixed_plane_angle\">The circle inside a fixed plane angle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polya%E2%80%99s_method_for_extremums&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: The circle inside a fixed plane angle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Circle_and_triangle.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/1/1a/Circle_and_triangle.png/400px-Circle_and_triangle.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"232\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/1/1a/Circle_and_triangle.png/600px-Circle_and_triangle.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/1/1a/Circle_and_triangle.png/800px-Circle_and_triangle.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let the plane angle with vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and the circle inside the angle be given. A straight line is drawn through point M of this circle, tangent to the circle and intersecting the sides of the angle at points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/792daa030c96693198f143f9176bf888b7ca25cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C.$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> Find the condition under which the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is the smallest.\n</p><p><i><b>Solution</b></i> \n</p><p>Let us imagine that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is a rod that cannot bend, and whose ends slide freely along the sides of the angle, and at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> it rests on a convex curve. \n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> be covered with a soap film, which, as usual, tends to reduce its area proportional to the energy. \nLet ABC be a section of a triangular prism, two faces of which AB and AC are fixed, and the third (BC) is a piston whose width can be changed so that it fits hermetically to the faces AB and AC and can slide along them. The piston rests on a cylinder whose cross-section is a circle with center O.\n</p><p>Air has been removed from the prism. External air presses on the piston and it comes to an equilibrium position. In this case, the energy of the system will be minimal if the volume of the prism becomes minimal.\nIn the equilibrium position, the moments of forces applied to the piston <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> on the segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/6/8068607716f3c2549af1dc978d77a422ca7201dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BM$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/5/4d5ee546f7ea84755eae7a58fbf1d892bd258118.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MC$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> are equal. \nThese moments are proportional to the square of the length of the segment, that is, the equilibrium condition is the equality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/8/cc83f3827b3cdac28752a0359ed12d585947734e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BM = MC$\" width=\"92\" height=\"12\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> (the center of mass of a homogeneous segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/f/b3f6f7cd16a47f33bebcf3fc361eeb808b630504.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC.$\" width=\"31\" height=\"12\" />)\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_sphere_inside_a_fixed_trihedral_angle\">The sphere inside a fixed trihedral angle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polya%E2%80%99s_method_for_extremums&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: The sphere inside a fixed trihedral angle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There is a sphere inside a fixed trihedral angle with vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/8207b79435a4bd499da7598fb44424c8a8499238.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> A plane is drawn through point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> of this sphere, tangent to the sphere and intersecting the edges of the angle at points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/6/716f38839110cdec891c16874f0d7e337a1751fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B, C,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/1/c61f1e8be9d73f590167b39417e42bbaa1787fe0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D.$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>Find the condition under which the volume of the pyramid <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is the smallest.\n</p><p><i><b>Solution</b></i> \n</p><p>Let us imagine the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/a/d7ada763e99852a6e44a15d46cce3c3709a944d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> in the form of the piston covering the evacuated volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/e/4ae54b43c219341de4a6e5919922cf55371e6ee3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" /> There is no friction of the piston against the walls.\n</p><p>In the equilibrium position, the volume of the evacuated part of the system is minimal. The equilibrium condition is the equality of the moments of pressure forces, which is equivalent to the equality of the moments of gravity for a homogeneous plate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/b/fcb9c01a2041b035894753449258b3754d538a5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCD.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> So the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> of the contact with the sphere (or any convex solid) must be at the center of mass of the polygon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/b/fcb9c01a2041b035894753449258b3754d538a5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCD.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_smallest_inscribed_equilateral_triangle\">The smallest inscribed equilateral triangle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polya%E2%80%99s_method_for_extremums&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: The smallest inscribed equilateral triangle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Rotation_triangle.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/72/Rotation_triangle.png/400px-Rotation_triangle.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"345\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/72/Rotation_triangle.png/600px-Rotation_triangle.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/72/Rotation_triangle.png/800px-Rotation_triangle.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A right triangle with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> be given. Find the area of the smallest regular triangle that can be inscribed in it. All vertices of the required triangle must be located on different sides of this triangle.\n</p><p><i><b>Solution</b></i> \n</p><p>Let's imagine the desired triangle as a cross-section of a drill drilling the plane of a given triangle. The axis of rotation does not have to be in the center of the desired triangle.\n</p><p>Let's turn the drill at a small angle clockwise so that the points of the drill touching the sides of this triangle move along the sides.\n</p><p>The required triangle is minimal, which means that all its vertices cannot lie on its sides. One or more will go inside. When turning counterclockwise the situation is similar.\n</p><p>This means that there is an instantaneous axis of rotation of the drill for such turns. Since the required regular triangle is pedal for a point on the instantaneous axis of rotation, this point is the first Apollonius point of given triangle.\n</p><p>Using the properties of this point, we find that the required area is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/2/ae233da8ba67875a63b6828039527a3b914e1148.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac {\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\frac { a^2 b^2}{a^2 + b^2 + \\sqrt{3}ab}.\\]\" width=\"152\" height=\"45\" />\n<b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Curve_of_the_smallest_length\">Curve of the smallest length</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polya%E2%80%99s_method_for_extremums&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Curve of the smallest length\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Shortest_line_in_triangle_B.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/b/b6/Shortest_line_in_triangle_B.png/280px-Shortest_line_in_triangle_B.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"280\" height=\"243\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/b/b6/Shortest_line_in_triangle_B.png/420px-Shortest_line_in_triangle_B.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/b/b6/Shortest_line_in_triangle_B.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Shortest_line_in_triangle_A.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f1/Shortest_line_in_triangle_A.png/320px-Shortest_line_in_triangle_A.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"320\" height=\"283\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f1/Shortest_line_in_triangle_A.png/480px-Shortest_line_in_triangle_A.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f1/Shortest_line_in_triangle_A.png/640px-Shortest_line_in_triangle_A.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Find the length of the curve of the smallest length dividing a given equilateral triangle with side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> into two equal parts.\n</p><p><i><b>Solution</b></i> \n</p><p>Note that the shortest (continuous) curve has ends <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> on two sides of the triangle, which we will call blue and red. Let us denote the common vertex of these two sides as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/5/b95fbfb49b675ba11392ba7c2603d0c87f89be76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Let us consider a set of six identical regular triangles in each of which the desired (identical!) curve is drawn.\n</p><p>We place the first of them in an arbitrary way in a certain plane.\n</p><p>We apply the second triangle to the first in the same plane so that the two blue sides and common vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> of these triangles coincide. In this case, the ends <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> of the curve on the blue line will coincide, that is, the set of these two curves is also a continuous curve.\n</p><p>We will apply the third triangle to the second so that the red sides, vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are coincide. We will similarly apply the fourth, fifth and sixth triangles.\n</p><p>As a result, we obtain a regular hexagon formed by sides opposite to vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/e/ccedc2f7ab3f66b36d3e0cf1536a3c801f01f421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" /> inside which a continuous curve limits an area whose area is equal to half the area of the hexagon. It is equal to the area of the three given triangles.\n</p><p>It is clear that this is a circle, its area (and radius) are known. The required length of the curve is a sixth of the length of its circumference.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/d/3fd179ab0bb1c61ac76027a3395ecdb00208b5bc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\pi r^2 = \\frac {3 \\sqrt {3}}{4} a^2 \\implies l = \\frac{\\pi r}{3} = \\frac {a \\sqrt{\\pi}}{2 \\sqrt[4]{3}}\\]\" width=\"271\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Triangle_of_the_biggest_area\">Triangle of the biggest area</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polya%E2%80%99s_method_for_extremums&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Triangle of the biggest area\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Max_area_triangle.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/d/d8/Max_area_triangle.png/300px-Max_area_triangle.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"194\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/d/d8/Max_area_triangle.png/450px-Max_area_triangle.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/d/d8/Max_area_triangle.png/600px-Max_area_triangle.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A fixed point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> in the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> be given. The distances from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> to the vertices of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f054be28a57624feee47965aad91fdc1b7a6792.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"16\" /> Find the condition that the area of triangle ABC is maximum.\n</p><p><i><b>Solution</b></i> \n</p><p>Let's imagine that at points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299ddf7c0f03ea46bf09f81f93adeea0756aa15c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" /> there are rods fixed on hinges that cannot bend or move out of a predetermined plane, but with minimal force they lengthen or shorten. The ends of the rods are connected in pairs in the form of a triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/9/2e9e8b2c23e0545ef6de81f0ea578173e9ab7a64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DEF$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> and slide freely together by changing the length of the rods.\n</p><p>Let there be a soap film located in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/9/2e9e8b2c23e0545ef6de81f0ea578173e9ab7a64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DEF$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> plane outside the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/9/2e9e8b2c23e0545ef6de81f0ea578173e9ab7a64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DEF$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> triangle, which, as usual, tends to reduce its area proportional to the energy.\n</p><p>The forces applied to the rod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/5/6d5c9afa92ae3180f02754c177e530d9d0f8b4d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> are directed perpendicular to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/2/3522e719f9c29395914efe40c2888d0fd88754aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DE,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"16\" /> therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/b/a7bcf0bfb84d412f07e2e71aadfaca3fbc26627f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BH \\perp DE.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"92\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>In the equilibrium position, the moments of forces applied to the rod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/5/6d5c9afa92ae3180f02754c177e530d9d0f8b4d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> on segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/b/a7baf95ecafc2d7791788ec79aeeae390d60b119.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> are equal, which means that В is the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/3/883839dc660034eb7a3172ac925f7bce4bd9de34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DE.$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>This means that the area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/b/c5ba7afa12298272441c17f763cea35fd1402bf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[DEF]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" /> is maximum if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the center of the circumcircle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/9/1a90b6a991ada84e21aafdc66864d7de295eb27b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\odot DEF.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>The area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/b/c5ba7afa12298272441c17f763cea35fd1402bf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[DEF]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times larger than the area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/b/ccbc7d181a37c7a074400d438c0bd53f646f57b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABC],$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" /> respectively, the area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/3/d33cc80fa8f093e155c5be46d2e5d9da3d7e1ef5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABC]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" /> is maximum if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the orthocenter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919b9f77a6985e760286799909be5bfe0152f40e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Clever_point_elephant_in_hollow_tetrahedron\">Clever point elephant in hollow tetrahedron</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polya%E2%80%99s_method_for_extremums&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Clever point elephant in hollow tetrahedron\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_1.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/6/68/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_1.png/300px-Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_1.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"247\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/6/68/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_1.png/450px-Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_1.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/6/68/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_1.png/600px-Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_1.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/8/84/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_2.png/300px-Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"211\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/8/84/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_2.png/450px-Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_2.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/8/84/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_2.png/600px-Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_2.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Tetrachedron_in_cyl.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/8/88/Tetrachedron_in_cyl.png/300px-Tetrachedron_in_cyl.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"78\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/8/88/Tetrachedron_in_cyl.png/450px-Tetrachedron_in_cyl.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/8/88/Tetrachedron_in_cyl.png/600px-Tetrachedron_in_cyl.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_3.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/fc/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_3.png/300px-Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_3.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"298\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/fc/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_3.png/450px-Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_3.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/f/fc/Tetrachedron_in_cylinder_3.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let the hollow regular unit tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> be given. A clever point elephant wants to fly along the shortest route that has points belonging to each face of the tetrahedron.\n</p><p>Find the length of this path.\n</p><p><i><b>Solution</b></i> \n</p><p>Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/9/a09d005043b64065d914babc24c11116c4166807.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = a.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"69\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/c/52cecdace5c5412c5be1d92ba11bcce7681f2e71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/5/1f5d96c8d1e481244e028c9e97d1d7b53206536b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DM$\" width=\"34\" height=\"12\" /> be the medians of the faces <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/b/00b778bf71f732ddeec20ae5fa5120ffe5f57d4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD. AM = \\frac {\\sqrt {3} a}{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"151\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/6/4a60d4239a9dc014670463164303473530802d36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {AE}{EM&#39;} = \\frac{DE_1}{E_1M} = \\frac {3}{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"156\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>It is known that the common perpendicular to these medians is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/2/f62ab5507a0e4d31e7e96e35e91959043221b2c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EE_1, EE_1 = \\frac {a}{\\sqrt{10}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"147\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>1. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/2/4329f4971786eca87230114320070effbfb57b59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE^2 = CM&#39;^2 + M&#39;E^2 = AE^2 + EE_1^2 = \\frac {37 a^2}{100}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"363\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/5/8a52f40afedf1c8727d71b0ad5a7ea0a7119b088.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> be symmetrical to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/9/459b08d5235f417c69f0cc0c2d473a4739abdb1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/0/710fb5fa3adedd7a16739e543d846747adbe05b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/b/4bb5885cc819f9a9c4fae71ac53613517259a405.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE_1 = AE_0 \\implies CE_0 \\perp EE_1, CE_0 = AE = DE_1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"401\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We similarly prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/f/ebfc915967e394fa7621f84eb284fa676cca4c1b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EE_1 = EE_0 = E_1E_2, BE_2 = AE.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"264\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>This means that all the vertices of the tetrahedron are located on circles of equal radius with centers on straight line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/6/85600ddab3050d2befa83da0dfdfba95fd1124ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EE_1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> and the distances between the centers of the circles are equal.\n</p><p>Consequently, the tetrahedron is inscribed in a cylinder <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/6/7c68f922d02b7109219fc9520f92fa42132417b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CYL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> with axis <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/0/710fb5fa3adedd7a16739e543d846747adbe05b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>2. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/1/921aade3100afbc11d0d13f71cdd7e68d83ad52e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varphi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"11\" height=\"11\" /> be the angle between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/b/4eb0f9b29950eeb3df108b64e4399ea0ba988f82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec {AE}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/6/836097e5e617364788bad26410c3bc8475ad109f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec {DE_1}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/f/28fc145dc5f8548af180e29d7af7aef687e5f198.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AD^2 = EE_1^2 + (AE^2 + DE_1^2 - 2 AE \\cdot DE_1 \\cdot\\cos \\varphi \\implies \\cos \\varphi = -\\frac{2}{3}\\]\" width=\"521\" height=\"37\" />   <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/1/d814884f694daa0f558688f94f946b7b599f290f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies  \\cos 2\\varphi = -\\frac{1}{9}.\\]\" width=\"141\" height=\"37\" /> \nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> be the center of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> face, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/2/7e299bb4ecad84c74a66b3036eb3a5478ebdfd27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IE_1 = \\frac{a}{10 \\sqrt{3}} = - CE_0  \\cos 2\\varphi.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"233\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>Let point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be symmetrical to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/a/8fa468d7c66ffe3fcee859e4ccded3a53a3dd367.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I, E_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> is symmetrical to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/9/459b08d5235f417c69f0cc0c2d473a4739abdb1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/8/2e8a25c3a526fcb582fb6b04c918ed6d3d05decd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/b/26bdd21c11ff2e4fd7ca11514975a5537d134dd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BF = a, IE_1 \\perp EE_1 \\implies F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"226\" height=\"18\" /> is located in the plane containing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/5/165dd2fc3942a9bf6b44cfc4544eb8cfcad404ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> perpendicular to the straight line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/5/9e59eb60d28288b5a923c19d64ce30d1ac77eb8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EE_1 \\implies FE_3 = \\frac {IE_1}{\\cos 2\\varphi} = CE_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"258\" height=\"43\" /> that is, this point lies on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/6/7c68f922d02b7109219fc9520f92fa42132417b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CYL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> and the tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/8/548da5c8c1eb94befe8cd8693224b098f14c64fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABFD = ABCD.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"147\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Similarly, we will construct an arbitrarily long chain of equal tetrahedra inscribed in a cylinder whose median faces contain a sequence of points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc09908c0435bc5b22f74467f02a39c333babc3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_i.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>It follows that the shortest path of the elephant cannot be shorter than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/9/4b95fa4e0fc6dd8a9fc909fa43823d7e93237536.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E_0E_3 = 4 EE_1 = \\frac {4a}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\]\" width=\"175\" height=\"41\" />\nThere are some such ways inside the tetrahedron. One of them is shown in the diagram.\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018103300\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.084 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.222 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 503/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 19591/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:27095:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018103300 and revision id 216186. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}